  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​812 — November 24, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193348/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">Read on the Web</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>JavaScript Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193285/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/zeicnfyzbo9kgkkmmgwf.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193285/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"lea.verou.me\">Nudeps: Bundler-Like Imports, None of the Baggage</a></span> — What if you could <code>npm install</code> a package and import it by name in the browser without a bundler? Lea and Dmitry Sharabin's new tool makes this easier than ever using import maps (which it keeps up to date). Run <code>npx nudeps install</code> and add the import map script to your HTML. There are also <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193286/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">demos</a> to try.</p>\n<p>Lea Verou </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 This work follows on from Lea's popular essay earlier this year: <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193287/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Web Dependencies are Broken. Can We Fix Them?</a></p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193284/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"110\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/d824ab56.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"110\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193284/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.meticulous.ai\">Agents Didn’t Clear Your Plate. They Dumped Reviews on It</a></span> — Your agents write PRs in minutes, then eat your afternoon in review. When did ‘saving time’ start to mean babysitting agents’ work? Meticulous shows the exhaustive impact of every PR, with no developer effort. Devs at Dropbox &amp; ElevenLabs have their afternoons back. You can too.</p>\n<p>Meticulous <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #993 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193288/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"blog.jquery.com\">jQuery 4.1 Released (Plus a Goodbye to 3.x)</a></span> — Most of us probably don't need it (just <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193289/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">16% of <em>State of JS 2025</em> respondents</a> use jQuery regularly) but it lives on in <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193290/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">~63% of sites</a>, so if that includes yours, both releases fix a selector bug (that can hang the page) and 3.8 is 3.x's last planned feature release.</p>\n<p>Timmy Willison (jQuery) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193291/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/ljwb1ir8f9txowyrlscx.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193291/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Chrome 157/158's DevTools can now debug with original variable and function names</a> <em>(see above)</em>, including in minified code, if the source maps include data from the <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193292/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">scopes proposal</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>TC39 meets again next week (remotely this time). <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193293/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">The agenda</a> has <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193294/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">text imports</a> up for stage 4, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193295/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">immutable <code>ArrayBuffer</code>s</a> for stage 3, and <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193296/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>AbortController</code>-based cancellation</a> for stage 2.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193297/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Ladybird</a>, a 'truly independent' browser built from scratch (including its JS engine), <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193298/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">is now in alpha</a>. Simon Willison <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193299/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">takes it for a spin here</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>RELEASES:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193300/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">webpack 5.113</a> – Building on its new <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193301/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">built-in HTML and CSS support</a>, webpack now scopes classes in &lt;<code>style module</code>&gt; blocks and updates matching names in the HTML.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🔒 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193302/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Bun 1.4.5</a> – A security release with fixes across HTTP/3, TLS, DNS and Zstandard, affecting <code>Bun.serve</code>, <code>fetch</code>, and several <code>node:</code> modules.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🖼️ <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193303/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Jimp 2.0</a> – Image processing in pure JavaScript. A huge release that now also supports animated GIFs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193349/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Nuxt 4.7</a>, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193304/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">DOMPurify 3.5</a>, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193305/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Redux Toolkit 2.15</a>, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193306/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">NestJS 12.6</a>, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193307/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">vlt 1.8.1</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>📖  Articles and Videos</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193308/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"blog.stdlib.io\">What Runs Your JavaScript? Engines vs Runtimes</a></span> — Defining where a language ends and its supporting runtime begins can be tricky, especially for JavaScript with its unusual history and genesis in the browser. Mara explains the key moving parts, the difference between a host runtime and JavaScript itself, and how <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193309/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">different JS implementations</a> draw the line in various places.</p>\n<p>Mara Averick </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193309/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">The JavaScript engines zoo</a> is worth a visit in its own right. It's a data table covering 139 JavaScript engines, their compliance, language they're built in, etc.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193310/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"developer-api.foxit.com\">Foxit Compares 10 eSignature APIs</a></span> — Embedded signing, webhooks, sandbox access, pricing and compliance, in one table you can shortlist from.</p>\n<p>Foxit <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #993 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193311/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.bencodezen.io\">Why I Switched to Vite+</a></span> — Is the unified <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193312/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">Vite+</a> toolchain worth adopting? Ben, of the Vue core team, shares his impressions of how it works on his 97,000 line project and concludes it's worth the effort.</p>\n<p>Ben Hong </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193313/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"svelte.dev\">Custom Renderers: Svelte Can Now Go Beyond the DOM</a></span> — Svelte components can now render to WebGL, native UIs, or the terminal, say, with a 'custom renderer' telling Svelte how to create/update nodes there. It's experimental in <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193314/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">Svelte 5.59</a> which just landed.</p>\n<p>Paolo Ricciuti and the Svelte Team </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 As an example of what this opens up, <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193315/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">gpuix-svelte</a> renders Svelte as a native desktop app using the Zed editor's <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193316/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">GPUI</a>. Yet another JS desktop/GUI app option!</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193317/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"rida.dev\">Bypassing iOS Safari's 60 FPS Limit for Canvas</a></span> — Safari famously caps page updates to 60 FPS by default, even on iPhones that go higher. But Rida found a sneaky way to get canvas rendering and touch input up to 120…</p>\n<p>Rida F'kih </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193318/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">To Remove Moment.js, We First Made Everything Depend on It</a> – Replacing ~1000 date-handling call sites without changing what users see across locales. <cite>Medhat Dawoud</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193319/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Chrome Prerendering is Weird</a> – It can run your site's JavaScript before you've even hit Enter in the address bar. <cite>Tom Bennet</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193320/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">What's New in Angular 22.3?</a> <cite>Cédric Exbrayat</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>🛠 Code &amp; Tools</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193321/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/uwcnyqvvnnq45bzzbyw6.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193321/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"component-party.dev\">Component Party: See What Changes in Solid 2</a></span> — The long-standing 'Rosetta Stone' of UI frameworks (well worth <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193322/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">revisiting in its own right</a>) now covers Solid 2, and you can see a 'before and after' of numerous code examples in 2 vs 1.</p>\n<p>Mathieu Schimmerling </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193323/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.solidjs.com\">Solid 2.0: The Long Hold</a></span> — Five years after 1.0, the signals-based UI framework is now stable with async built right into its reactivity graph (so return a promise from a computation and everything downstream just handles it). Ryan explains the thinking, and why the <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193324/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">SolidStart</a> metaframework has been retired.</p>\n<p>Ryan Carniato </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 Ryan expands on the latter point in <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193325/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">The Metaframework is Dead. Long Live the Metaframework</a>, a 20-minute ViteConf talk.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193326/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"fandf.co\">Build Durable AI Agents &amp; Workflows in TypeScript</a></span> — Open-source and fault-tolerant. Tasks pick up right where they left off, even through failures, restarts, or deploys.</p>\n<p>Trigger.dev <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #993 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193327/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"vlt.io\">vlt Opens Its npm Package Mirror to Everyone</a></span> — Clean installs can spend a lot of time downloading package metadata, but vlt's registry <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193328/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">serves <em>much less</em> of it</a> giving installs that can take 20-70% less time. No account/token is needed anymore, so give it a spin and see if it suits your setup.</p>\n<p>Erick Wendel (vlt) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193329/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">Astrobook: A Minimal Storybook Alternative</a></span> — An alternative to the popular UI component playground that supports React, Vue, Preact, Svelte, Solid, Lit, and Astro. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193330/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\">The demo site</a> includes multiple framework examples.</p>\n<p>Ocavue </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>🕹️ <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193331/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">melonJS 20.17</a> – Popular 2D game engine. Isometric and hexagonal Tiled maps are now drawn by the GPU.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193332/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Replay Node 26</a> – A 'time-travel' build of Node.js (for Linux x86-64 only) that records a run so you can debug it after the fact.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>📈 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193333/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Frappe Charts 2.3</a> – Simple, zero-dependency SVG charts. Adds axis titles.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🎵 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193334/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">OpenSheetMusicDisplay 3.0</a> – A way to render MusicXML sheet music.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193335/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">jest-dom 8.0</a> – Custom DOM matchers for Jest and Vitest (5+ only).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193336/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Svelte Flow 2</a> – Node-based UI library.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📸 Ditch the native app: Add QR, Data Matrix and EAN scanning to your web app with STRICH. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193337/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Free 14-day trial, cancel anytime</a>.</p>\n<p>🚀 <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193338/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">SpreadJS v20 is coming!</a> Preview the major release of the industry-leading JavaScript spreadsheet at our Dec. 3 PM <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193338/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Office Hours livestream</a>.</p>\n<p>🎬 Reviewing AI PRs? <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193339/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">TWD</a> adds a video of each new test to the PR, so you see the UI change without checking out the branch. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193339/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Free and MIT</a>.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0;\"><p>📢  Elsewhere in the ecosystem</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193340/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/dddhcwgalhztlpdfjavs.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 100%;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193340/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">React Anatomy</a> <em>(above)</em> is a DevTools extension that splits a running React page into 3D layers showing its components along with live props. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193341/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">There's a demo</a> you can try before installing anything.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🔒 Hijacked GitHub accounts are being used to <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193342/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">exploit servers that aren't patched against a variety of React and Next.js flaws</a> and publish stolen credentials publicly. <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193343/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">Here's some of what to check</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🗳️ <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193344/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">The <em>State of JavaScript 2026</em> survey</a> closes soon, so take it if you can. Browser developers pay attention to the results, so it's worth doing (plus the way they structure the survey means you can learn about various JavaScript features along the way).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>📊 Speaking of surveys, Devographics has also just published <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193345/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">the <em>State of HTML 2026</em> results</a>. Kilian Valkhof <a href=\"https://javascriptweekly.com/link/193346/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #3366aa; font-weight: 500;\" target=\"_blank\">sums them up here</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
